course: dictionary
lesson: 11412
title: Words for building up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Accumulated means gathered or collected bit by bit over time. Something that accumulates grows slowly, one small piece at a time, until there is a lot of it. «Snow accumulated on the window ledge overnight.» The snow did not land all at once in a heap; it built up gradually during the night.
explain: The word can also describe a total. «The accumulated points showed Maya had won the game.» Here accumulated describes all the points added together. Ask yourself: has something been building up bit by bit? If yes, accumulated may fit.
explain: Everyday words can replace it: built up, piled up or collected. Try swapping one in. «Snow built up on the window ledge overnight» still makes sense, so built up is a good match. The formal word is useful in maths, science and money lessons.
fact: Accumulate means to gather or collect bit by bit over time.
fact: «Accumulated» is the past form of «accumulate», and it can also be an adjective describing a total that has built up.
fact: In maths, accumulated can mean a running total, such as «She accumulated 120 points over the week.»
fact: In science and geography, dust, snow and rubbish can accumulate.
fact: In money lessons, savings and interest accumulate over time.
exercise: «Dust had accumulated on the bookshelf over the summer.» What does accumulated mean here? | Blown away in one gust | Painted bright blue | Cleaned off quickly | *Built up bit by bit over time
exercise: In «Rubbish accumulated behind the bins», which everyday phrase fits best? | Flew away at once | Was sorted into boxes | *Piled up | Was washed clean
exercise: Ali checked his accumulated points at the end of the week. What does accumulated describe? | A wrong answer on a test | *The total that has built up | A single point won in one game | A dropped ball
test: «The rain accumulated in the bottom of the bucket.» What is happening to the rain? | Being poured out | *Gathering bit by bit over time | Drying up quickly | Turning into ice at once
test: Which everyday words are good replacements for accumulate? | Cleaned away, lost or thrown out | Shouted, sung or whispered | *Built up, piled up or collected
exam: In «Savings accumulate in the bank each year», what does accumulate mean? | Disappear suddenly | *Gather bit by bit over time | Are spent all at once | Are counted once a month
